{"kind":"report","text":"G5 architectural assessment — bounded read-only result for request #355. No gate pass or production adoption.\n\nRecommendation: keep the planned independent strfry retention backend, place a small independently operated membership-aware gateway in front of it, and make the backend unreachable by clients. This is the smallest mechanism change from Plan 001 that can express newcomer history access and removal at both historical and live delivery boundaries. A write plugin or immutable recipient tags cannot supply that policy. The remaining consequential decision is what \"current\" means during a partition. If globally current transport membership is required, use fail-closed fresh authorization with a revocation/send barrier; do not claim primary-outage reads work without an independent authority or fencing arrangement. The named-prefix alternative is a workable, smaller availability-oriented policy only if its older-history exposure after an unseen removal is explicitly accepted. It must not be relabelled globally current.\n\nI verified builder assertion #354 (533d64645ce6401436ab8744a9192a866db07e1b) against actual pinned source. I read the governing Plan 001 at e5f1b276a04c7849791749db5763f4f5c0c5991c and the current landed crypto note at c254887d4cb365bbc7ce6a8fe8b1959e34328be5. The actor-owned protocol-feasibility worktree contains unrelated uncommitted P1c work; I did not edit it, its caches, or any candidate source. No native relay, gateway, dependency installation, cloud service or real-key test was run by me. All runtime behavior proposed below is UNEXECUTED; source implications are identified separately. Root's native dependency work is independent.\n\n1. What is already promised, and what still needs a profile decision\n\nPlan 001 lines 299-316 require explicit primary/mirror reader policies, declared public metadata and an adversary-by-asset table. P5 lines 449-477 require read/write checks at historical and live boundaries, AUTH/removal tests and a reviewed gateway/alternative if bare strfry is insufficient. The crypto note lines 184-202 and G5 row 217 preserve opaque action/private-definition/secret content and require a declared reader/history policy. These documents do not themselves freeze an exact globally-current-versus-named-prefix freshness contract; that is a G5 decision. Full newcomer history is also a chosen interval/closure contract under G2, not something Nostr tags automatically grant.\n\nFour different authorities must remain distinct:\n- NIP-42 proves possession of an authenticated Nostr key on a connection; it is not membership.\n- The gateway must derive transport read permission from a verified public owner-authorized admission/control history tied to instance/genesis and the named accepted sequencing boundary. It cannot infer actual encrypted MLS membership by decrypting controls, and must not treat a bare sequencer signature as owner admission authority.\n- MLS epoch removal controls future decryption. It does not stop an old member fetching old ciphertext or erase previously learned plaintext/keys.\n- Publicly declared metadata may be exposed intentionally, but that declaration cannot silently expand to every event ID, count, participant, instance or historical routing tag just because a mirror lacks read ACLs.\n\n2. Exact pinned strfry result\n\nSource: strfry 4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10; the local source HEAD and clean status were verified. ReadRestrictor.h:94-118 sends unrestricted kinds to anyone; restricted kinds require AUTH; with restrictReadToInvolvedPubkey=true the authenticated key must be the event author or occur in an immutable p tag. Turning that flag off admits any authenticated key, not just an instance member. Historical REQ uses this predicate at RelayReqWorker.cpp:11-21; historical-to-live catch-up and live broadcasts use it at RelayReqMonitor.cpp:31-46,60-78. No owner roster/version is an input. [Pinned reader predicate](https://github.com/hoytech/strfry/blob/4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10/src/ReadRestrictor.h).\n\nConsequences are direct source implications, not claims of executed defects: adding Carol later cannot make her match old signed p tags; removing Bob cannot make his old author/p matches disappear. Adding future recipients requires new signed events, changing identities/closure, while deleting old events breaks retention. Even a perfect immutable-recipient implementation cannot provide \"all current members may retrieve all retained instance history; removed readers lose subsequent service access.\" The documented event-sifter and router plugins decide storage/upstream transfer, not each downstream reader. [Plugin contract](https://github.com/hoytech/strfry/blob/4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10/docs/plugins.md).\n\nAdditional exact-pin paths must be tested or disabled; I did not execute these:\n- COUNT: ReadRestrictor.h:50-80 skips a filter with no kinds at line 56. RelayReqWorker.cpp:12 bypasses event filtering for count-only and lines 24-39 return the query total; QueryScheduler.h:104 uses matched identities. With COUNT enabled and an allowed unscoped filter, COUNT {} or an ids-only restricted-event query can therefore reveal counts without the per-event reader predicate. The default filter validation is disabled. This is a source-grounded test hypothesis, not a reproduced runtime exploit. [Reader code](https://github.com/hoytech/strfry/blob/4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10/src/ReadRestrictor.h), [COUNT output](https://github.com/hoytech/strfry/blob/4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10/src/apps/relay/RelayReqWorker.cpp).\n- Reconciliation: RelayNegentropy.cpp:167-175 filters the in-memory query result, but a matching configured persistent negentropy tree is reconciled directly at 216-221 and 260-266 without that predicate. Reconciliation exposes set/ID information even when EVENT content is blocked. The prefilter's AUTH check is not a membership check. Keep public NEG disabled until both paths and continuations pass dedicated fixtures. [Pinned reconciliation](https://github.com/hoytech/strfry/blob/4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10/src/apps/relay/RelayNegentropy.cpp).\n- AUTH: RelayIngester.cpp:272-279 normalizes URLs to the lowercased authority, discarding path/scheme, and its session accepts one key. Do not use this as the gateway's exact instance-path binding or advertise full pinned NIP-42 multi-key behavior on that basis. NIP-42 at the pinned revision permits multiple authenticated pubkeys and gives challenge/URL/time checks; the gateway must implement its claimed semantics itself. [strfry AUTH](https://github.com/hoytech/strfry/blob/4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10/src/apps/relay/RelayIngester.cpp), [NIP-42](https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/42.md).\n\n3. Mechanism comparison\n\n| Option | What it supplies | Remaining work and verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Independent gateway + network-private pinned strfry | Arbitrary owner-authorized instance reader policy without changing retained signed events; keeps planned backend/export tooling | Recommended mechanism. Gateway must own AUTH, every read/write route, control persistence, outbound revocation and bounded query handling. It is an authorization service, not a transparent WebSocket proxy. |\n| Relay/framework with existing hooks | Can put the same policy beside storage and fan-out | Plausible alternative, not already certified. Still needs the same authoritative membership/freshness state, every-path checks and tests. No freshness problem disappears by changing the relay. |\n| Immutable author/p recipient policy | Fits the bare predicate | Different policy: newcomers lack old history and removed historical recipients retain access. Not a preserving fix. |\n| Public ciphertext or \"any AUTH key\" | Very little implementation | Exposes historical metadata and ciphertext to a wider audience. Encrypted content alone does not satisfy the requested reader boundary. Do not choose this just to make conformance green. |\n\nFor a concrete hook candidate I inspected the distributed source of fiatjaf.com/nostr/khatru at v0.0.0-20260902034142-316ef6591fa2, underlying Git revision 316ef6591fa2f1d4247ce949a9361a44dcfe1f95 (Go proxy origin metadata). Its actual API has OnRequest, a replaceable QueryStored iterator, OnCount, OnAuth and PreventBroadcast. responding.go:16-33 checks the request then writes each yielded history event; listener.go:382-393 checks PreventBroadcast before live output. Therefore history needs a membership-aware iterator, not just a subscription-open check, and revocation/send races still need a defined linearization/queue policy. ForceBroadcastEvent deliberately bypasses PreventBroadcast; exclude it and management/export bypasses. I inspected source bytes in memory, did not install or run the framework. It is a programmable candidate, not a turnkey proven ACL relay. Source hashes: relay.go 31d8ca7b1d10b1845944d58e0054440287eaeaf14e072f74f7e7d9137c55331a; responding.go b8c053bf00b73c03b54a982bbbf676abe6191a64f1c339dd5d2907ec6ddd0cd3; listener.go bcba2081da4b454012c2a8534ec4fbfbf8601409b805e31774c40fefbfaea7b0. [Module documentation](https://pkg.go.dev/fiatjaf.com/nostr/khatru), [exact distribution metadata](https://proxy.golang.org/fiatjaf.com/nostr/@v/v0.0.0-20260902034142-316ef6591fa2.info).\n\n4. Minimum gateway boundaries\n\nOne independent mirror host can run the gateway, a small persistent control/coverage database, and strfry bound only to loopback/private namespace. Public access terminates at the gateway. Firewall both IPv4/IPv6, alternate ports/hosts/paths, HTTP upgrade variants, debug/admin/export endpoints and container host-port publication. A hidden DNS name is not isolation. Only the gateway and explicitly trusted local administrator/backup/replication processes can reach the backend. Those operators can bypass reader ACLs and see retained metadata/ciphertext; they must still receive no content-decryption or recovery keys. A malicious operator or authorized member can copy bytes already visible to it. This is an explicit trust limit, not solved by AUTH.\n\nEnroll a genesis-to-owner/sequencer/gateway binding through a trusted operator or authenticated invitation/owner registration flow. Persist verified public owner control signatures, previous control/version links, accepted sequencer proof and the G3 binding to the encrypted transition's identity. Unknown owner/genesis, rollback, gaps, incompatible halves and observed forks must not grant access. The gateway evaluates transport authority from public data only; a successful AUTH or a claimed encrypted MLS epoch is insufficient.\n\nDo not require the newcomer to read the private journal before the gateway can learn the newcomer's admission. The invitation supplies authenticated root/instance/mirror context, while the gateway ingests the signed public control/proof through its internal replication/control path. A narrowly bounded proof-submission endpoint can carry that closure if required; it grants nothing merely because a caller supplies it. No public roster-enumeration endpoint is needed. If the join boundary has not reached the gateway's authorized verified state, the newcomer waits or gets an unavailable/restricted result, rather than receiving a blanket bootstrap bypass.\n\nOnce a newcomer is authorized for full history at the chosen authority boundary, event age, old recipients and old authors do not restrict access to the promised retained closure. The gateway supplies historical signed bytes and encrypted definitions/assets/archive objects; G2's separately authorized recovery-key delivery determines decryption. Removal must cancel continuing history pagination and live subscriptions, discard unsent application queues, and stop new authorized output under the selected policy. Already released/in-flight bytes and previously cached plaintext are not revocable; define the server's send/enqueue boundary explicitly. Re-authenticating the same removed key does not re-admit it.\n\n5. The essential policy/freshness decision\n\nA signed prefix, EOSE, a recent timestamp, a successful AUTH and a cached owner record each fail to prove that there is no unseen removal. A mirror separated from the primary cannot distinguish \"primary is stopped, no new controls\" from \"primary continues behind a partition and accepted a removal.\" This is an information/authority limitation, not a strfry configuration detail.\n\n| Candidate policy | History and live rule | Primary-outage availability and exposure |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Globally current, fail closed | Authoritative fresh public membership plus a defined serialized revocation/send boundary; do not issue new output when authority/fencing freshness is unavailable | Preserves the stronger read contract under its declared honest authority assumptions. If authority is only the primary, independent outage history reads stop; a once-per-session check or stale cached token is insufficient. |\n| Bounded authorization lease | Signed grant identifies instance, subject, authority version, expiry and maximum scope; known revocation cuts it off, expiry closes output | Allows only the stated bounded outage. An unseen removal may still permit older-history reads until expiry plus bounded in-flight effects. A lease becomes strict only if removal cannot become effective until outstanding grants are revoked/drained or expire. This is a protocol change, not \"fresh current membership\" by naming. |\n| Membership at a named retained prefix F | Derive admission from the gateway's highest verified contiguous retained prefix F; serve only bytes/closure at or below F; apply intervening controls before advancing authorization and every live delivery | Supports outage retrieval of F without a primary call. It makes no latest-tip or unseen-revocation claim. It is the smallest availability-oriented alternative, but it weakens globally current removal semantics. |\n| Strict current + continued offline mirror reads | All serving gateways participate in a revocation/fencing barrier, or a separate authoritative control service supplies equivalent guarantees | Can preserve both aims conditionally, but adds coordination/authority. Membership change availability falls when a serving gateway cannot acknowledge or be safely fenced. This must be reviewed with G3/P4, not smuggled into G5. |\n\nFor the named-prefix option requested by root, consider mirror F=40 where Bob is admitted; primary F=41 removes Bob, but that suffix is withheld from the mirror. Bob can still AUTH to the mirror and retrieve previously unfetched history, old definitions/archive ciphertext and metadata through 40. If he retains relevant historic keys, this may newly disclose old plaintext to him. Serving nothing beyond 40 prevents that mirror from releasing new suffix content but does not prevent this older-history exposure. A newcomer admitted only at 41 is denied until the gateway can verify that admission boundary. This option preserves ciphertext integrity, content secrecy against parties lacking keys, and reader authorization AS OF 40; it does not preserve globally current metadata/history access against Bob.\n\nThe prefix policy must use the gateway's monotonically highest known verified authority prefix, not a prefix chosen by the caller. Once removal is known at 41, Bob cannot request \"history as of 40\" to regain access. A history cursor captured at 40 is not a membership lease: a known removal cancels further pages even if their content belongs to 40. Advancing F requires contiguous public control verification before any new live byte is released. Forks, rollback, missing dependencies or unverifiable authority stop advancement/reads as appropriate; an out-of-order suffix must never leak while buffered. A trusted newer revocation/fencing notice may conservatively deny earlier; never ignore known revocation simply because the data backfill lags.\n\nOne preserving strict design to investigate, if both current membership and primary-outage reads are mandatory: fix the serving gateway set for the prototype, and require a removal's public control to be durably denied by every serving gateway (with its old output queues closed/fenced) before the sequencer can make the removal effective. Additions grant only after the accepted public control proof is available. That lets an isolated gateway retain a safe roster because effective removals cannot pass it silently, but it intentionally blocks removal progress while the gateway is unreachable. Gateway replacement, old URL retirement, lease expiry alternatives and stale storage restores require explicit fencing. A restored old control database must be read-disabled until reconciled with an independent monotonic authority; an old signed checkpoint alone cannot prove freshness. This is an unexecuted possible design with real G3/P4 costs, not my adoption recommendation.\n\n6. Query, discovery, write and retention surface\n\nThe minimum public gateway profile should allow only the documented AUTH/REQ/CLOSE/EOSE/EVENT response behavior and the fixed experimental kinds/filter fields selected by G4. It should reject broad/cross-instance filters before backend work, validate EVERY OR branch, and check the resulting event's verified instance/identity independently of the query. Never forward arbitrary JSON, ids-only queries, COUNT, NEG-OPEN/NEG-MSG, management APIs or HTTP export directly to the backend. Initially disable public COUNT, HLL and reconciliation entirely; their result sets/IDs/counts are metadata too. Internal replication may use separately authenticated bounded tools without granting that interface to readers.\n\nGateway NIP-11 must describe its actual capabilities/units and limits, not echo the backend's supported-NIP list. An instance-specific path is allowed when it changes behavior; bind the gateway's AUTH challenge to the exact canonical behavioral URL, time window and connection. Handle the pinned NIP-42 multi-key semantics if claiming that NIP. Keep public discovery generic to the declared service metadata; do not publish rosters, retained counts, instance lists or existence-sensitive differences unintentionally. Remaining network timing/size and operator routing exposure must be declared. [NIP-01 endpoint/filter/EOSE semantics](https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/01.md), [NIP-11](https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/11.md).\n\nWrites/replication need their own authenticated channel and verified genesis/sequencer/owner provenance. A read member is not automatically a journal publisher. Keep signed retained event bytes unchanged, verify their canonical identity, and require normal retained kinds/retention independent of replaceable discovery. The gateway can use a narrow private ingestion endpoint and a private backend write policy as defense in depth. No arbitrary public client writes, deletions or replaceable-event tricks should remove journal/closure history.\n\nNIP-70 is publication-by-event-author protection, not read membership or a genesis registry. If a retained outer event carries [-], a generic third-party replicator authenticated under a different key cannot validly republish it through the NIP-70 EVENT path. Do not strip the tag or silently bypass the rule. Either have the actual outer author publish to each destination, or explicitly choose a replicable normal-event private-ingestion profile whose confidentiality rests on the reviewed gateway/operator boundary, without claiming NIP-70 protection for that path. Privileged offline import/restore is a separately declared operator capability and not evidence of compliant author-authenticated publication. [NIP-70](https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/70.md).\n\nFor completeness, capture a verified signed tip and retrieve original identities/predecessors plus the full retained closure using bounded ID backfill or a clearly separate Noseq export API. EOSE does not prove a complete prefix, nor freshness; timestamp-only paging loses equal-timestamp records under limits. Keep data coverage, authority prefix, holes and latest-tip knowledge as separate values. Retry bounded slices with durable cursors; slow consumers close/resync rather than accumulating memory. A candidate tiny fixture profile can cap a connection at four subscriptions/one filter each, 64 IDs per fetch, and a 2 MiB outbound queue, with the final G4 event/frame cap and byte-bounded pages. These are suggested probe values, not adopted defaults; all catch-up work must be resumable rather than claiming success at a resource cap.\n\n7. Minimum runnable fixture contract to build next — NOT EXECUTED here\n\nUse actual pinned strfry behind the proposed gateway, a real WebSocket client, real synthetic Nostr signatures, a tiny signed public-control/journal fixture and a separate authority/replication process whose link can be cut. Do not use predicate-only mocks as the conformance result. Every case retains source/config/runtime identities, sent/received raw frames, expected IDs and authority/data frontiers. No script with these cases exists merely because it is listed here.\n\nG5-F01 Bare baseline: historical/live author/p access, newcomer denied old events, removed old recipient still served; COUNT {} and ids-only COUNT; both on-demand and configured-tree NEG paths. Record actual pin behavior including any deviations from the source hypotheses.\nG5-F02 Boundary/bypass: unauthenticated, wrong key, wrong instance/path, replayed/expired challenge, foreign genesis/control, mixed OR filters and unsupported commands yield no restricted events/counts/IDs. Direct backend IPv4/IPv6/port/HTTP/admin access from a client network must fail; NIP-11 matches public capabilities.\nG5-F03 Bootstrap/newcomer: owner-authorized admission reaches gateway internally before the reader can access history. An admitted newcomer retrieves all named old journal/definition/archive identities despite absent historic p tags; a caller-created or incomplete invitation cannot grant access.\nG5-F04 Removal/history/live race: revoke during paginated history, queued live output, reconnect and re-AUTH. No newly authorized output after the selected cutoff; already released bytes are measured as in-flight. Apply known controls before the next live event and do not let an old cursor downgrade authorization.\nG5-F05 Freshness split, with DIFFERENT expected outcomes by chosen policy: hide removal 41 from mirror 40. Strict-current mode refuses new output when freshness/fencing cannot be established. Prefix mode may serve Bob old history \u003c=40 and must label that exposure and frontier; it serves no 41+ bytes and denies the newcomer admitted only at 41. A later observed removal denies Bob even on requests for 40. Lease mode tests exact expiry, clock skew bounds, known revocation and queued-output cancellation. Do not count the prefix-mode old-history exposure as a globally-current pass.\nG5-F06 Recovery/rollback: restart control state; restore a stale database and lose the authority link. Strict mode remains fenced; prefix mode must not forget a previously persisted newer removal. Test control gaps/forks, only one half of admission/MLS binding and bounded catch-up. Fail with an explicit unavailable/partial state rather than declaring full coverage.\nG5-F07 Byte retention/completeness: more same-timestamp events than the query cap, a missing middle identity, definition/archive closure holes and writes during fixed-tip backfill. Retrieve and verify exact signed bytes, deduplicate historical/live overlap, and distinguish EOSE from complete coverage.\nG5-F08 Write/replication and resources: unauthorized append/delete/foreign sequencer rejected; exact-author protected publication accepted only under the selected supported route; wrong-author protected replication rejected. Exercise byte/frame/filter/queue limits, slow readers, bounded retry and authenticated private restore/readback.\n\nG5 may approve the explicitly chosen profile and isolated executable feasibility/conformance fixtures only after their evidence is reviewed. P5 separately must prove the actual primary DO and independently operated mirror deployment implements it, including service isolation, durable revocation state, hibernation/retry, retention/restore and primary-disabled independent retrieval. Neither a source audit nor successful bare strfry startup establishes those results.\n\n8. Decisions that genuinely block adoption\n\nA. Select and name globally-current, bounded-lease or named-prefix reader authority, and the newcomer history interval. Explicitly ratify any older-history exposure after an unseen revocation; do not infer consent from a wish for outage availability.\nB. Freeze the public G3 control/owner/genesis binding and membership/data frontier rules, bootstrap channel, rollback/fork handling and AUTH subject mapping. If strict current plus outage reads is required, choose and test the independent authority/fencing arrangement and its membership-change availability cost.\nC. Build and test every public gateway path, network isolation, persistence and queue/revocation boundary; decide whether a hook framework reduces work without introducing bypass routes.\nD. Resolve actual event framing/retention/replication including NIP-70 handling, then run the real fixtures. Root's native dependency feasibility work is a prerequisite for local strfry execution, not a reason to weaken the profile.\n\nExact reference inventory: strfry 4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10; NIPs c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb (01/11/42/70 read locally at exact HEAD); Plan001 e5f1b276a04c7849791749db5763f4f5c0c5991c; crypto note c254887d4cb365bbc7ce6a8fe8b1959e34328be5; alternative hook source 316ef6591fa2f1d4247ce949a9361a44dcfe1f95. All recommendations are architectural inputs for the builder's separately reviewed proposal. No G5 pass, production adoption, installation or runtime conformance claim is made.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true"}}